    "markdown": "# Project Closeout and Termination\n\n\n\n::: {.callout-note}\nThis chapter is in preparation. It will be built from **Lecture 12 -- Project\nCloseout and Termination** of MGMT-3105, following the same structure as\n[Chapter 1](01-Sustainability.qmd) and\n[Chapter 9](09-Cost-Estimation-and-Budgeting.qmd).\n:::\n\n## Learning objectives (planned)\n\nBy the end of this chapter you will be able to:\n\n- Distinguish the ways a project ends: completion, extinction, addition, integration, starvation.\n- Run an orderly administrative closeout: deliverable acceptance, contract closure, financial closure.\n- Release resources and transition the product to operations or the customer.\n- Facilitate a lessons-learned review and write to the organizational knowledge base.\n- Explain the organizational and human implications of closing a project (team dispersal, morale).\n- Recognise when to terminate a troubled project early, and how to do it responsibly.\n\n## Topic outline\n\n1. Types of project termination.\n2. The closeout checklist: scope verification, punch list, sign-off.\n3. Contract and procurement closure.\n4. Financial closure and final cost accounting.\n5. Lessons learned and the post-implementation review.\n6. Resource release and team transition.\n7. Early termination: decision criteria and stakeholder communication.\n8. Running case: closing out the RC-vehicle prototype project and handing off to manufacturing.\n",
